<MethodLimits noun="position sizing math">
  <Fragment slot="method">Every run starts at $10,000 and is 100 trades long. Each trade risks the same share of the account as it stands then. A win adds that amount times the payoff; a loss takes it. Trades are independent and the odds never change. A run stops if the account falls to $5,000 or lower. Otherwise it ends after 100 trades, and it is ahead if it ends above $10,000. The shares are the exact odds over every possible run, not a simulation. The fastest-growing size is the win chance, less the loss chance divided by the payoff.</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="limits">Real trades do not have fixed odds or fixed payoffs, and one trade's result can affect the next. The figures cover 100 trades and say nothing past them. Half the start is one line among many a trader could draw. Commissions, taxes and slippage are left out. The examples are samples, not anyone's record. This page does not use your accounts or positions.</Fragment>
</MethodLimits>
